learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T06:28:29.626Z","harness:claude-code","Two capabilities can each be individually correct and jointly false. Scoping the merge fence to persisted project schema was the right fix for a real local-versus-CI disagreement, and registering item types through extensions is the mechanism by which the product claims to serve any domain. Composed, they mean that using the customization capability silently disables the durability capability, and the drift detector reports clean because it is asking whether the fence matches the schema rather than whether every item document is covered. Coverage questions should be asked about the population, not about the declaration."
files[2]{path,scope,note}:
  .gitattributes,project,"committed fence: twelve built-in folders plus stories,with jsonl and json catch-alls but no toon catch-all"
  src/sdk/merge/install.ts,project,buildMergeAttributePatterns receives project-schema type folders only; extension-contributed type folders never reach it
tests[1]{command,scope,timeout_seconds}:
  pm merge install --dry-run --json,project,240
docs[1]{path,scope,note}:
  docs/MERGE_SAFETY.md,project,documents the field-aware merge guarantee whose coverage this gap breaks for extension-typed items
body: "## Reproduced 2026-07-31 in a clean sandbox at HEAD\n\n```\ngit init; pm init\npm install <repo>/packages/pm-digital-twin --project      # registers TwinAsset / TwinFacility\npm create --title \"Pump A\" --type TwinAsset --status open  # -> pm-2d6j\nls .agents/pm/                                             # twin-assets/ twin-facilities/ now exist\npm merge install --dry-run | grep -c twin                  # 0\n```\n\nThe item is written to `.agents/pm/twin-assets/pm-2d6j.toon`. `pm merge install --dry-run` emits 28 patterns covering the twelve built-in type folders plus the JSONL/JSON catch-alls, and **zero** patterns matching `twin-assets` or `twin-facilities`. It reports `changed: false`, i.e. the drift detector considers the fence complete.\n\nThe same hole is present in this repository right now. Five item directories exist on disk with no fence coverage: `cards`, `changesets`, `twin-assets`, `twin-facilities`, `refs` — the folders for Card, Changeset, TwinAsset, TwinFacility and VcsRef, all of which `pm stats` enumerates under `by_type`. `pm merge install --dry-run` against the live workspace declines to add any of them and reports clean.\n\nBy contrast `stories` — a **project-schema** custom type registered in `.agents/pm/schema/types.json` — is fenced. So the generator does read project-schema types. It does not read extension-contributed types.\n\n## Why the gap exists\n\n`buildMergeAttributePatterns` (`src/sdk/merge/install.ts:247`) takes `typeFolders` as an argument and its own doc comment states it is \"the single coverage contract shared by pm merge install and the validate merge-fence-drift detection ... so schema-added custom types can never silently diverge from the installed fence without a warning.\" The caller now supplies **only persisted project schema folders**, by design: pm-mkzw1x deliberately separated project merge-type resolution from active extension registrations so that default validation would stop recommending a change the required CI gate reverses.\n\nThat determinism fix was correct and should not be reverted. But pm-mkzw1x closed with the acceptance clause \"tests cover tracked project profiles and optional runtime-only profiles **without weakening custom item merge protection**\", and that clause is not satisfied: extension-typed items now have no merge protection at all, and the detector reports clean rather than reporting a gap.\n\n## Consequence\n\nThe two capabilities the product leads with cancel each other. Universal customization says any project models its own domain by registering item types through an extension. Multi-agent branch safety says concurrent agents merge their tracker writes field-aware and lose nothing. The moment a project uses the first, its items fall back to git default line-based merge on a TOON document for the second. Two branches that each edit a different field of the same twin asset produce a conflicted or silently mis-merged file, which is exactly the failure the driver exists to prevent — and no diagnostic anywhere says the protection is absent.\n\nThe fence has `**/*.jsonl` and `**/*.json` catch-alls. It has no `**/*.toon` catch-all, which is why item documents alone are exposed.\n\n## Candidate fix that preserves the determinism property\n\nAdd a tracker-wide `\"<root>/**/*.toon\" merge=pm-item-toon` pattern (and the matching `**/*.md`) ahead of the per-folder rules. Git applies last-match-wins, so the explicit per-folder rules still take precedence and the committed fence stays a pure function of the project schema — no extension state enters it — while any folder an extension creates is protected by default. Coverage then does not depend on knowing the type set in advance, which is the property a universal tool needs.\n\nAlternative, if a catch-all is judged too broad: keep the fence project-scoped but make the absence loud — `pm health` and `pm validate` report unfenced item folders that contain items, naming the extension that registered each type."
